basis-link-247c68f0.interpretation:
This is the defining transformation law for a covariant vector $V$. The components $V'_{\mu}$ in a new coordinate system $x'$ are related to the components $V_{
u}$ in the old system $x$ via the transformation matrix whose elements are $ rac{\partial x^{
u}}{\partial x'^{\mu}}$. This matrix is the Jacobian of the transformation from $x'$ to $x$. The indices $\mu$ and $
u$ run over the dimensions of the space or spacetime, and the repeated index $
u$ implies a summation over all its possible values (Einstein summation convention). This transformation law ensures that the scalar product $V_{\mu} A^{\mu}$ (the contraction of a covariant and a contravariant vector) is an invariant scalar, independent of the coordinate system.

